# One render path

This is the invariant the whole app rests on. Break it and the preview stops predicting the
file, which is the only thing the preview is for.

## The rule

`IFrameRenderer.Draw(session, context)` is the single entry point. The preview applies a
letterboxing transform before calling it; the encoder applies none. Nothing else differs.

A renderer therefore must not:

- ask how large the control is, or whether one exists;
- branch on being in a preview;
- read the app's theme, the Windows accent colour, or anything about the machine;
- keep state between calls.

If a renderer needs something, it comes through `FrameContext`. If `FrameContext` cannot carry
it, that is the design decision to make — not a second parameter passed only on one path.

## Progress, never time

`Draw` receives `Progress`, a fraction from 0 to 1. It must be a pure function of it: the same
fraction produces the same pixels, every time, in any order.

This is what lets the encoder run faster than real time. The tools this replaces could not,
because `MediaRecorder` timestamped frames as they arrived, so the animation advanced with the
wall clock and a ninety-second video took ninety seconds to write. A renderer that reads a
clock, counts its own frames, or assumes it was called for frame *n−1* puts that limitation
back, and the symptom is a file whose pacing does not match its preview.

`Playback` is wall-clock on purpose, because a preview answers "is this too fast to read". That
is the one place a clock belongs. It drives `Progress` and nothing else.

## Anything preview-only lives in the preview

The safe-area guides are drawn by `PreviewSurface` after the renderer returns. That is not
tidiness — it is why they cannot reach a file: the encoder does not own a `PreviewSurface`.

A flag on the renderer saying "do not draw the guides this time" would be the same guarantee
resting on somebody remembering to clear it. If something must appear in a preview and never in
a file, it goes on the preview side of the call. No exceptions, because the failure mode is a
published video with diagnostic overlays on it.

## Baseline pixels, always

Every measurement is written against 1080×1920 and multiplied by `FrameContext.Scale`, or taken
through `FrameContext.Px`. No drawing code contains a resolution.

A font size or an offset assigned directly is correct at 1080p and wrong everywhere else, and
that reads as a layout bug rather than as a missing multiplication. When adding a renderer,
grep it for bare numbers that are not fractions before believing it.

## A geometry question is answered once

`FrameContext` computes `ChartLeft`, `ChartRight`, `Baseline`, `ChartHeight` and `TitleTop`. A
renderer reads them; it does not re-derive them from `Margins` and `Height`.

Two renderers each working out where the baseline sits is two answers free to disagree, and the
one that drifts is whichever is edited second. `StageRenderer` exists for the same reason: the
grid, baseline, title block and credit are drawn once for both indicators.
